{
  "evaluation": "Under the strict rules, `apparent` is limited to two cases: an underpowered/pilot null study, or findings that do not measure the same thing (whereas different depression scales and timings are explicitly classified as `genuine` with `outcome_measure`). Responses A and C follow this rule by classifying the pair as `genuine` with `population` as the primary reason (acute stroke vs hospitalized COVID-19 patients) and `outcome_measure` and `design_or_bias` in `also_differs`. Response A is slightly better than C because it also notes the critical nuance from Study B's claim field—that the direction of the association was not actually stated in Study B's abstract despite the 'harm' label. Response B is ranked last because it stretches the `apparent` verdict beyond the two permitted categories and misapplies `outcome_measure` to cover an unreported direction of effect.",
  "ranking": [
    "A",
    "C",
    "B"
  ]
}
